        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-275408",
        ":question/problem": "Calculate $C+D-E$, where\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,E=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n| $C+D-E=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Performing the operations on each corresponding entry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C+D-E & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3+2-6 & 5+(-4)-5 \\\\ 5+4-(-4) & -1+1-2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -4 \\\\ 13 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
